WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:02.400
Hello! You're watching Mostly Weekly

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:05.000
and I am unfortunately Andrew Heaton.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.780
Puerto Rico, it's Spanish for 'rich port'

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:09.930
which is sort of ironic because it's bankrupt.

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:11.780
Kinda like how Iceland isn't full of ice

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:13.579
and Greenland isn't very green

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:16.520
and Lonely Island has lots of fans.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:18.000
Islands are liars!

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.660
But now poor puerto is even less rico.

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:23.220
A month ago the vicious category 4 hurricane
Maria

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:26.860
ripped through the island razing a 50 to 60
mile swath of destruction.

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:30.000
But the worst part of this tragedy, and I
think we can all agree on this,

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.640
is that we didn't-

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:33.240
appreciate Donald Trump
enough.

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:39.560
But luckily, Puerto Ricans have been spared the
onslaught

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:42.440
of Donald Trump's category 5 man-baby tweet
storm.

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:43.160
Why?

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:48.200
Because 90% of Puerto Rico is still without
power and 88% are without cell reception.

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:51.300
And it's gonna be at least 6 months before
power is restored.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:53.060
How is this even possible?

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:57.859
To answer that question we have to rewind
the clock 5 months ago to May 3rd,

00:00:57.859 --> 00:01:00.510
or two White House Communication Directors
ago,

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:02.890
when Puerto Rico filed for bankruptcy.

00:01:02.890 --> 00:01:05.420
It couldn't pay the 70 billion dollars it
owed to creditors,

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:08.960
and that's because the country's been in economic
decline since 2006.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:11.640
So to answer why Puerto Rico looked like this,

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:14.100
and now looks like this,

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:18.160
we have to ask: 'Why the island's been in
economic freefall since 2006?'

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:19.280
and the answer is:

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:23.000
Government, that's my final answer.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.820
Puerto Rico is without power for three reasons.

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:30.400
We raised taxes, which led to a deficit, which
led to a brain drain, which led to a deficit.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.010
The United States and Puerto Rico had been
flirting for years

00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:34.640
since the Spanish American War.

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:37.240
Then the United States finally put a ring
on it in 1952

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:39.920
and Puerto Rico became a US commonwealth,

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:42.840
and they decided to raise a couple of beautiful
industries together.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:46.060
So the United States showered Puerto Rico
with corporate tax breaks.

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:50.680
But then the honeymoon phase ended in 1996,
the US phased out those tax breaks.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:53.400
Businesses fled the island and to make up
the difference,

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:56.240
the Puerto Rican government started binge
eating debt.

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:00.270
So faced with a whopping 70 billion dollar
debt to Wall Street creditors,

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:02.630
many Puerto Ricans bailed for the mainland.

00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:05.120
The Puerto Rican economy has been so wrecked

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:09.030
that there are now more Puerto Ricans living
outside Puerto Rico than in.

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:11.000
Bad news for Puerto Rico, but:

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.660
great news for
the New York Puerto Rican Pride Parade.

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:15.460
This results in a brain drain

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:18.860
and this labor exodus makes paying back a deficit extremely hard.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:22.620
It's like trying to fill a hole, but all you
have is more holes.

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:25.340
We made imported goods more expensive.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:27.400
In 1920 the US passed the Jones Act,

00:02:27.409 --> 00:02:32.180
which requires all goods shipped between US
ports to be transported by US vessels.

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:37.500
Meaning that Puerto Rico depends on the US
ships, which make imports way more expensive.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:42.220
Which is cool 'cause it's not like Puerto
Rico imports 84% of it's food, right?

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:43.760
Ha! 'cause that'd be crazy!

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:48.300
After the Hurricane, President Trump suspended the Jones Act

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.860
with the break neck reaction speed of-

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:52.420
six days, but hey!

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:55.600
He had more important kneeling-related matters
to attend to.

00:02:55.610 --> 00:03:00.099
However residents are still suffering from
years of artificially high import prices.

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:04.320
So even though 43.5% of residents live below
the poverty line,

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:06.120
it's still crazy expensive to live there.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:06.720
Don't believe me?

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:07.280
Pssst!

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:10.820
Hey buddy, wanna buy some sugar?

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:14.320
Only 40% more than mainland prices.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:17.040
PREPA, Puerto Rico's publicly run electrical
grid,

00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:19.750
adopted a hipster approach to electricity,

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:22.909
complete with dilapidated facilities and vintage
technology.

00:03:22.909 --> 00:03:24.860
Instead of using modern energy sources,

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:27.840
Puerto Rico still generates two thirds of
it's power through burning

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:29.600
good old-fashioned petroleum,

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:33.220
which is very steam punk and retro and dumb.

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:33.900
Why?

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:38.880
because all that oil has to be imported and
requires a skilled workforce.

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:41.010
So what happens when you have to import expensive
oil

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:45.760
and handle it with a workforce that's decreased
by a third over the past five years?

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:46.320
This.

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:49.600
But there's hope.

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:54.660
In a state of emergency the Puerto Rican government
can bypass permits

00:03:54.670 --> 00:03:56.620
and immediately revoke regulations.

00:03:56.620 --> 00:04:00.439
Also, private citizens are turning to the
black market to put the lights back on.

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:03.680
In an ongoing effort to become the Batman,

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:06.360
Elon Musk has offered to save Puerto Rico's
electrical grid

00:04:06.370 --> 00:04:08.420
with batteries and solar power.

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:12.600
Making Puerto Rico the Lois Lane of electrical
grids.

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:15.300
But the more important thing is that Puerto
Rico is without power

00:04:15.310 --> 00:04:17.590
and it was an avoidable catastrophe.

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:19.660
We shouldn't have to reach a state of emergency

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:23.300
to discover the devastating effects of taxation
and excessive regulation.

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:27.300
And because Puerto Rico is without power,
people are going to die from heat stroke,

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:31.540
the clean water supply is restricted and the
supply of food and drugs has shrunk.

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:32.420
Worst of all:

00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:34.600
we didn't sufficiently appreciate Donald Trump.

